//? Title: The Apriori Algorithm: Mining Frequent Itemsets and Association Rules
//? Subtitle: Downward closure, why the prefix join misses nothing, and who survived the Titanic, with datamunge's algorithms::apriori_frequent_itemsets
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Frequent-itemset mining finds every combination of items that occurs in at
least a minimum number of records. Association rules turn these
combinations into statements such as "first-class women survived". Apriori
(Agrawal & Srikant, 1994) makes the search feasible with one fact: every
subset of a frequent set is frequent. We prove this, and prove that
Apriori's prefix join generates every frequent set, so the pruning loses
nothing. We then mine the 891 Titanic passengers in `dm::datasets::titanic()`,
each described by class, sex, age group, family, port and survival. At a
minimum support of 5%, Apriori counts 463 candidate itemsets and finds 333
frequent ones. Eclat and FP-growth return the same 333 sets. The strongest
rule is \(\{\)first class, female\(\}\Rightarrow\) survived, with confidence
0.968 and lift 2.52. All 53 rules about survival with confidence at least
0.8 pass Fisher's exact test after Benjamini-Hochberg correction. Only 17
of them beat every shorter rule by at least 0.02 in confidence.
)

\toc

> The data: 891 passengers as transactions

`dm::datasets::titanic()` lists 891 passengers of the Titanic with their
ticket class, sex, age, relatives aboard, port of embarkation and whether
they survived. To mine it, each passenger becomes a *transaction*: a set of
items of the form `column=value`. Age is grouped into child (under 18),
adult and unknown (177 passengers have no recorded age). Family is "with"
if the passenger travelled with any sibling, spouse, parent or child.
`algorithms::encode_transactions` turns the chosen columns into item sets.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic()
      | dm::mutate("Survived", [](const auto& r) { return std::string(r.get_double("Survived") > 0 ? "yes" : "no"); })
      | dm::mutate("Age", [](const auto& r) {
          return std::string(r.is_null("Age") ? "unknown" : r.get_double("Age") < 18 ? "child" : "adult"); })
      | dm::mutate("Family", [](const auto& r) {
          return std::string(r.get_double("SibSp") + r.get_double("Parch") > 0 ? "with" : "alone"); });
  std::print("{}\n", passengers | dm::select({"Name", "Pclass", "Sex", "Age", "Family", "Embarked", "Survived"})
                                | dm::slice_head(4) | dm::to_md());

  const auto enc = alg::encode_transactions(passengers, {"Pclass", "Sex", "Age", "Family", "Embarked", "Survived"});
  const auto singles = alg::apriori_frequent_itemsets(enc.transactions, 1);
  std::print("\n{} transactions, {} distinct items.\n\n| item | support | share |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n",
             enc.transactions.size(), enc.labels.size());
  std::vector<std::pair<std::string, std::size_t>> items;
  for (const auto& f : singles)
    if (f.items.size() == 1) items.emplace_back(enc.labels[f.items[0]], f.support);
  std::ranges::sort(items);
  for (const auto& [label, count] : items)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {:.1f}% |\n", label, count, 100.0 * count / enc.transactions.size());
}
```
