Two embarkation records are missing, so those two passengers carry five
items instead of six. Survival is the item of most interest: 342 of the
891 survived (38.4%).

> The method

>> Support, confidence and lift

For a database of \(N\) transactions, the *support* \(s(I)\) of an itemset
\(I\) is the number of transactions that contain every item of \(I\). The
itemset is *frequent* if \(s(I)\ge\text{minsup}\). A rule \(A\Rightarrow C\),
with \(A\) and \(C\) disjoint, is scored by

$$
\text{conf}(A\Rightarrow C)=\frac{s(A\cup C)}{s(A)}=\hat P(C\mid A),\qquad
\text{lift}(A\Rightarrow C)=\frac{\text{conf}(A\Rightarrow C)}{s(C)/N}=\frac{\hat P(A\cap C)}{\hat P(A)\,\hat P(C)} .
$$
\alttext(The confidence of A implies C is s of A union C over s of A, the estimated probability of C given A. The lift is the confidence divided by s of C over N, which equals the estimated probability of A and C over the product of the estimated probabilities of A and of C.)

Confidence is a conditional probability. Lift compares it with the base
rate of \(C\): lift 1 means \(A\) tells us nothing about \(C\).

>> Downward closure

**Lemma.** If \(J\subseteq I\) then \(s(J)\ge s(I)\).

*Proof.* Every transaction that contains all of \(I\) contains all of
\(J\). \(\square\)

So support can only fall as an itemset grows. If any subset of a candidate
is infrequent, the candidate is infrequent and need not be counted. Apriori
searches level by level. Let \(L_k\) be the frequent \(k\)-itemsets, with
items kept in sorted order:

1. Count every single item to get \(L_1\).
2. *Join*: for each pair \(X,Y\in L_k\) that agree on their first \(k-1\)
   items and have \(x_k<y_k\), form \(X\cup\{y_k\}\).
3. *Prune*: drop any joined candidate that has a \(k\)-subset outside
   \(L_k\).
4. Count the survivors in one pass over the database. The frequent ones
   form \(L_{k+1}\). Stop when \(L_{k+1}\) is empty.

>> Why the join misses nothing

**Claim.** Every frequent \((k+1)\)-itemset is produced by the join.

*Proof.* Let \(Z=\{z_1<\dots<z_{k+1}\}\) be frequent. Remove its last item
to get \(X=\{z_1,\dots,z_k\}\), and remove its second-to-last item to get
\(Y=\{z_1,\dots,z_{k-1},z_{k+1}\}\). Both are subsets of \(Z\), so both are
frequent by the lemma and lie in \(L_k\). They agree on their first
\(k-1\) items, and \(z_k<z_{k+1}\). So the join forms
\(X\cup\{z_{k+1}\}=Z\). All \(k\)-subsets of \(Z\) are frequent, so the
prune step keeps it, and the count confirms it. \(\square\)

The join also never forms the same candidate twice, because \(Z\)
determines \(X\) and \(Y\). Apriori therefore finds every frequent itemset
and only counts candidates whose every subset is frequent. Eclat and
FP-growth, in the same header, compute the same collection by intersecting
transaction-id lists and by growing a prefix tree. They make a useful
cross-check.

> Mining the passengers

The next block runs Apriori at three minimum supports: 5%, 10% and 20% of
the passengers, which is 45, 89 and 178. `apriori_levels` reports, for each
itemset size, how many candidates the join produced, how many survived the
prune and were counted, and how many were frequent.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/apriori_levels.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto passengers = dm::datasets::titanic()
      | dm::mutate("Survived", [](const auto& r) { return std::string(r.get_double("Survived") > 0 ? "yes" : "no"); })
      | dm::mutate("Age", [](const auto& r) {
          return std::string(r.is_null("Age") ? "unknown" : r.get_double("Age") < 18 ? "child" : "adult"); })
      | dm::mutate("Family", [](const auto& r) {
          return std::string(r.get_double("SibSp") + r.get_double("Parch") > 0 ? "with" : "alone"); });
  const auto enc = alg::encode_transactions(passengers, {"Pclass", "Sex", "Age", "Family", "Embarked", "Survived"});
  const auto& tx = enc.transactions;

  std::print("| minsup | k | joined | counted | frequent |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::vector<double> ks, joined, counted, frequent;
  for (std::size_t minsup : {45, 89, 178}) {
    std::size_t total_counted = 0, total_frequent = 0;
    for (const auto& l : alg::apriori_levels(tx, minsup)) {
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", minsup, l.k, l.joined, l.candidates, l.frequent);
      total_counted += l.candidates;
      total_frequent += l.frequent;
      if (minsup == 45) {
        ks.push_back(l.k);
        joined.push_back(l.joined);
        counted.push_back(l.candidates);
        frequent.push_back(l.frequent);
      }
    }
    const auto a = alg::apriori_frequent_itemsets(tx, minsup);
    const bool same = a.size() == alg::eclat_frequent_itemsets(tx, minsup).size() &&
                      a.size() == alg::fp_growth_frequent_itemsets(tx, minsup).size();
    std::print("| {} | total | | {} | {} (Eclat and FP-growth agree: {}) |\n", minsup, total_counted,
               total_frequent, same ? "yes" : "no");
  }
  std::vector<double> kj, kc, kf;
  for (double k : ks) { kj.push_back(k - 0.27); kc.push_back(k); kf.push_back(k + 0.27); }
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
      .bars(kj, joined, "joined", dm::RGB{203, 213, 225}, 0.26)
      .bars(kc, counted, "counted", dm::RGB{96, 165, 250}, 0.26)
      .bars(kf, frequent, "frequent", dm::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 0.26)
      .y_limits(0, 330)
      .title("Apriori level by level, minsup = 5%")
      .x_label("itemset size k")
      .y_label("itemsets")
      .size(860, 480)
      .save_svg("build/apriori_levels.svg");
}
```
