// result_end

\caption(Every rule with confidence at least 0.8 from the frequent sets at 5% support, by support and confidence. The colour runs from pale grey at lift 1 to dark blue at lift 2.6 and above.)
\alttext(Scatter plot of confidence from 0.8 to 0.98 against support from 0.05 to about 0.53. Most of the 227 points are mid blue-grey and crowd at support below 0.13; the rest spread out to the right at lower lift. The darkest, high-lift points all have support below about 0.11 and lie at every confidence from 0.8 to 0.98.)

Every one of the 53 survival rules is overwhelmingly significant. With 891
passengers and a 38% base rate, a confidence above 0.8 is far from chance.
Significance is not the problem here; redundancy is. Most rules restate a
shorter one with an extra item that changes little. For example,
\(\{\)first class, female, adult\(\}\Rightarrow\) survived has confidence
0.974, barely above the 0.968 of \(\{\)first class, female\(\}\). With a
threshold of 0.02 on improvement, 17 rules remain, and they tell the known
story:

- Women in first and second class survived at 97% and 92%, more than
  twice the base rate. The improvements are large (+0.23 and +0.18)
  because "female" alone gives only 0.74.
- Men did not survive: 81% of them died, and the rule
  \(\{\)male\(\}\Rightarrow\) died improves on the base rate of 0.62 by 0.20.
  Third-class men died at 87%, and adult men in second class at 93%.
- Passengers with no recorded age died more often: 52 of the 177 survived
  (29%), against 38% overall. Missingness carries information here too.

Lift above 1 on its own is a weak filter. All 227 rules in the scatter
have lift at least 1.12, yet many are near-duplicates. Improvement asks
the sharper question: does this rule tell us anything its shorter
versions do not?

> Summary

- Support can only fall as an itemset grows, so a candidate with an
  infrequent subset can be skipped. The prefix join generates every
  frequent \((k+1)\)-set exactly once, from its two subsets that drop one
  of the last two items. Apriori is therefore complete.
- On the Titanic passengers at 5% support, the pruning cut the joined
  candidates from 697 to 463 counted itemsets, of which 333 were frequent,
  out of 1 727 itemsets the attributes allow. Eclat and FP-growth found
  the same 333.
- The survival rules repeat the known story, women and children first and
  class mattering, and every high-confidence one is statistically
  significant. Most are near-copies of shorter rules, so improvement or a
  similar redundancy filter is essential before reading a rule list.

```{cpp, eval="no"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
namespace alg = dm::algorithms;

auto enc   = alg::encode_transactions(table, {"Pclass", "Sex", "Survived"});  // column=value items
auto sets  = alg::apriori_frequent_itemsets(enc.transactions, /*min_support_count=*/45);
auto work  = alg::apriori_levels(enc.transactions, 45);                        // joined / counted / frequent
auto rules = alg::association_rules(sets, enc.transactions.size(), /*min_confidence=*/0.8);
auto gain  = alg::rule_improvements(rules, sets, enc.transactions.size());
std::print("{}\n", alg::itemset_label(rules[0].antecedent, enc.labels));
```
